WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
There is a catch, though, and it is worth knowing about.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:10.200
Sometimes the direction is ambiguous — here the value in one cell could have come from the left or from above.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.275
That gives a second, equally good alignment, shown in green.

4
00:00:14.275 --> 00:00:17.900
Nothing inside the algorithm can choose between them.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.725
You have to bring something else to bear.

6
00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:29.775
In this case the green alignment puts a gap, then a residue, then a gap, which almost never happens in evolution — so the yellow one is the better bet.

7
00:00:29.775 --> 00:00:35.300
The argument differs case by case, and it is outside what the algorithm can tell you.

8
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:42.625
Smith-Waterman, for local alignment, differs in one way: no negative numbers are allowed in the matrix.

9
00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:53.050
So the zeroth row and column are filled with zeros rather than accumulating gap penalties, and anywhere the recurrence would give a negative number, you write zero instead.

10
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.950
Filling the matrix under those rules gives this.

11
00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.900
The traceback differs too.

12
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.550
Instead of starting at the bottom right, you start at the highest number anywhere in the matrix.

13
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:11.200
And you trace back by the same rules as before, but you stop when you hit a zero.

14
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.500
What comes out is the best local alignment of the two sequences.

15
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.625
Note where the score is now: at the cell where the traceback began, not at the corner.

16
00:01:21.225 --> 00:01:28.300
Both algorithms needed a score for matching one residue against another, so now we need to say where those come from.

17
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.575
The simplest scheme makes the score depend only on which two amino acids are involved.

18
00:01:33.575 --> 00:01:40.700
There are twenty, so there are four hundred possible pairs — a twenty-by-twenty table, called a substitution matrix.

19
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:48.225
In the simplest case it is symmetric: scoring alanine against histidine is the same as histidine against alanine.

20
00:01:48.225 --> 00:01:49.675
Then gaps.

21
00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:55.900
A gap needs a penalty, and the penalty depends on how many consecutive gaps you are introducing.

22
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.225
Three schemes are in use.

23
00:01:58.225 --> 00:02:04.375
A linear penalty, which is what the examples used, is simply proportional to the number of gaps.

24
00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:19.575
The trouble is visible in the top alignment on the slide: it produces gaps alternating with residues, and it fails to line up the QQW that both sequences contain — which could easily be read as those proteins not sharing a functional site when they do.

25
00:02:19.575 --> 00:02:22.450
An affine penalty fixes that.

26
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:30.500
It has two parts: a large cost to open a gap, around ten, and a much smaller cost to extend one, around nought point one.

27
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:36.650
That pushes gaps into a few long stretches instead of many short ones, as in the second alignment.

28
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:47.725
And if you know in advance that some region should not contain gaps — because it is a rigid part of the structure, say — you can raise the penalty just there, with a position-specific factor.

29
00:02:47.725 --> 00:02:52.975
That is an advanced technique; from here on this lecture uses affine penalties.

30
00:02:53.575 --> 00:02:56.900
There are two families of substitution matrix.

31
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:04.000
PAM stands for accepted point mutations — the letters rearranged because PAM is easier to say than APM.

32
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.950
Margaret Dayhoff introduced it in the nineteen-seventies.

33
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:15.550
BLOSUM is the blocks substitution matrix, developed in the nineteen-nineties by Steven and Jorja Henikoff.

34
00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:21.175
The next slides go through how each is built, because the difference matters when you choose one.

35
00:03:21.775 --> 00:03:29.800
Dayhoff worked from thirty-four protein superfamilies, aligned pairwise with Needleman-Wunsch and assembled into multiple alignments.

36
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:36.925
Some families are extremely conserved — the dehydrogenases at the top, where only a few positions differ at all.

37
00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:44.500
Others are not: the kappa-caseins at the bottom have only a handful of conserved positions and some highly variable ones.

38
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:50.800
At the time, the Atlas of Protein Sequence and Structure held about eight hundred proteins in total.

39
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:58.175
Several groups later repeated the calculation on much more recent data, and the results came out essentially the same.

40
00:03:58.775 --> 00:04:06.750
From those families she built seventy-one phylogenetic trees and inferred the ancestral sequences — the internal nodes.

41
00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:15.025
Then she counted substitutions: between an observed sequence and its predicted ancestor, or between ancestors at different levels.

42
00:04:15.025 --> 00:04:18.725
The result is the substitution matrix on the right.

43
00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:23.600
It is symmetric by construction, and the reason is worth understanding.

44
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:30.400
Nearly all the sequences come from species alive today, so you cannot tell which direction a mutation went.

45
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.275
The residue before the change cannot be distinguished from the residue after it.

46
00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:39.675
The diagonal is left empty, because those are not mutations.

47
00:04:40.275 --> 00:04:48.550
Next she worked out how often each of the twenty amino acids occurs: the count of that residue divided by the total number of residues.

48
00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:55.175
The graph shows the result for her dataset, with a recent UniProt release alongside for comparison.

49
00:04:55.175 --> 00:04:59.875
The point of the dashed line is that amino acids are not used at random.

50
00:04:59.875 --> 00:05:03.600
If they were, each would appear five per cent of the time.

51
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.200
They do not.

52
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:15.500
Then the relative mutability of each residue: how often it was seen to change, divided by how often it appears at all — that is, how often it could have changed.

53
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:19.525
The numbers are scaled so that alanine comes out at a hundred.

54
00:05:19.525 --> 00:05:24.050
The thing to notice is that mutability does not track frequency.

55
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:30.575
The residues are in the same order as the previous graph, where frequency fell steadily within each group.

56
00:05:30.575 --> 00:05:32.500
Here it does not.

57
00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.825
Now the mutation probability matrix at one PAM.

58
00:05:36.825 --> 00:05:42.625
One PAM means an evolutionary distance at which one per cent of residues have changed.

59
00:05:42.625 --> 00:05:52.600
Note that this is a measure of change, not of time: a conserved family like the dehydrogenases takes far longer to accumulate one per cent than the caseins do.

60
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:58.275
One PAM corresponds roughly to sequences that are eighty-five per cent identical.

61
00:05:58.275 --> 00:06:04.125
The off-diagonal entries give the probability of one residue mutating into another.

62
00:06:04.125 --> 00:06:16.300
The diagonal comes from a different argument: each column lists all twenty possible outcomes for a residue, it either stays or becomes one of the other nineteen, and those probabilities must sum to one.

63
00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.575
So the diagonal is whatever is left over.

64
00:06:20.175 --> 00:06:25.300
Part of the resulting PAM1 matrix, with its properties listed on the right.

65
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:35.575
It is not symmetric, even though the substitution counts were — because each amino acid has its own frequency, and dividing by different numbers breaks the symmetry.

66
00:06:35.575 --> 00:06:39.825
There is a different symmetry instead, in the equation at the bottom.

67
00:06:40.425 --> 00:06:43.100
The two extremes are worth seeing.

68
00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:52.575
At zero evolutionary distance nothing has changed, so PAM0 is a diagonal matrix: every residue stays itself with probability one.

69
00:06:52.575 --> 00:07:00.475
At infinite distance everything has changed, and the only thing left governing which residue you find is how common that residue is.

70
00:07:00.475 --> 00:07:06.975
So every column becomes identical, and each is simply the frequencies of the twenty amino acids.

71
00:07:06.975 --> 00:07:11.150
That is the background distribution, and we will use it shortly.

72
00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:17.950
Now the trick that makes the whole family practical: PAM2 comes from PAM1 by multiplication.

73
00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.625
Think of a system with three states — red, green, blue.

74
00:07:21.625 --> 00:07:26.200
After one step, there is one way to still be red: nothing happened.

75
00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.650
After two steps there is another way: it changed and changed back.

76
00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:39.875
So the probability of being red after two steps is red-stays-red twice, plus red-to-green times green-to-red, plus red-to-blue times blue-to-red.

77
00:07:39.875 --> 00:07:46.325
Sum over all the intermediate possibilities, for every entry, and you have matrix multiplication.

78
00:07:46.325 --> 00:07:49.325
PAM2 is PAM1 times itself.

79
00:07:49.325 --> 00:07:51.975
Multiply again for PAM3.

80
00:07:51.975 --> 00:07:55.925
The PAM250 matrix was built exactly this way.

81
00:07:56.525 --> 00:08:00.025
One more step turns probabilities into scores.

82
00:08:00.025 --> 00:08:06.050
Statistics gives us the odds ratio: what was observed, divided by what chance would give.

83
00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:12.125
If the ratio is one, the event is no more likely than chance and needs no explanation.

84
00:08:12.125 --> 00:08:16.425
Below one it is rarer than chance, above one it is commoner.

85
00:08:16.425 --> 00:08:24.375
Taking the logarithm is more convenient, because then "rarer than chance" is simply a negative number, whatever base you use.

86
00:08:24.375 --> 00:08:29.450
The top of the slide shows the odds ratio for PAM matrices at any distance.

87
00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:39.125
The bottom shows the PAM250 mutation matrix turned into the PAM250 log-odds matrix, with a factor of ten in front and logarithms base ten.

88
00:08:39.725 --> 00:08:42.700
BLOSUM takes a different route.

89
00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:56.500
The Henikoffs scanned the BLOCKS database for highly conserved regions of protein families — regions aligned without any gaps at all — and counted how often each residue occurred and how often each substitution did.

90
00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:01.700
From that they computed a log-odds score for each of the two hundred and ten possible pairs.

91
00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:06.975
The key difference: every BLOSUM matrix comes from observed alignments.

92
00:09:06.975 --> 00:09:09.475
Nothing is extrapolated.

93
00:09:10.075 --> 00:09:13.650
Four differences, then, and they are worth remembering.

94
00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:20.050
PAM rests on an explicit model of evolution, counting replacements along the branches of a tree.

95
00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:25.400
BLOSUM counts them straight from the alignment, with the model left implicit.

96
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:32.000
PAM counts mutations across a whole global alignment, conserved regions and variable regions alike.

97
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.400
BLOSUM uses only highly conserved, gap-free blocks.

98
00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:42.850
PAM needs only one matrix derived explicitly; everything else is matrix multiplication.

99
00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:51.000
Every BLOSUM matrix has to be derived separately — you cannot get BLOSUM80 from BLOSUM40.

100
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.300
And the numbering runs in opposite directions.

101
00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.475
A higher PAM number means a greater evolutionary distance.

102
00:09:58.475 --> 00:10:03.950
A higher BLOSUM number means greater similarity, and therefore less distance.

103
00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:12.950
PAM150 is for more distant sequences than PAM100; BLOSUM62 is for closer sequences than BLOSUM50.

104
00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:20.800
The most interesting question in this area is how much change can accumulate before homology stops being detectable.

105
00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.100
The graph gives the answer.

106
00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:31.675
Beyond about two hundred and fifty PAM, sequence identity falls below twenty per cent and alignments stop being statistically convincing.

107
00:10:31.675 --> 00:10:35.175
Past that point, sequence alone is not enough.

108
00:10:35.175 --> 00:10:40.600
You need profile hidden Markov models, or structure, and those come later in the course.

109
00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:43.875
Which brings us to significance.

110
00:10:43.875 --> 00:10:49.225
Once you have an alignment and a score, you need to know whether that score means anything.

111
00:10:49.225 --> 00:10:56.625
The usual approach is to compare it against scores from sequences known not to be homologous — a background distribution.

112
00:10:56.625 --> 00:11:01.800
You can build one by shuffling the residues of the original sequences at random.

113
00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:08.975
If that background is genuinely random it follows a Gaussian distribution, with a mean and a standard deviation.

114
00:11:08.975 --> 00:11:12.400
One technical note on the standard deviation.

115
00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:16.250
If you have the entire population, you divide by N.

116
00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:25.650
If you have only a sample, you divide by n minus one, which makes the spread slightly larger to reflect that a sample tells you less than the whole population does.

